<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Kutai</h1><p class="st-meta">350 CE – 1635 CE (approx.) · capital Muara Kaman</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The earliest documented kingdom in Indonesia, known from King Mulavarman's Sanskrit pillar inscriptions of around 400 at Muara Kaman in East Kalimantan.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Seven stone yupa, sacrificial posts inscribed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pallava-script">Pallava script</a>, were found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muara-kaman">Muara Kaman</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahakam-river">Mahakam River</a>. They name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kudungga">Kudungga</a>, his son Asvavarman, called the founder of the dynasty, and grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mulavarman">Mulavarman</a>. Kudungga's name is not Sanskrit, suggesting a local chief whose descendants adopted Indian kingship and ritual.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The inscriptions praise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mulavarman">Mulavarman</a> for giving thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> and for performing Vedic sacrifices, showing a court shaped by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> ideas of kingship. Its position on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahakam-river">Mahakam</a> allowed it to gather forest products and gold from the interior. After the inscriptions almost nothing is recorded for centuries.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The later history of Kutai Martadipura is known mainly from the chronicles of its rival, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kutai-kartanegara-sultanate">Kutai Kartanegara Sultanate</a>, which conquered it in 1635 and merged the two under the name Kutai Kartanegara ing Martadipura. The yupa inscriptions are held in the National Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-kutai.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Kutai" width="320" height="283"><figcaption>Image: DDG9912 · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Special_Region_of_Kutai.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>350 CE – 1635 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Muara Kaman</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kudungga, Asvavarman, Mulavarman</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sanskrit (inscriptions)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kutai Martadipura, Kutai Martapura, Kutai</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=992&amp;focus=kingdom-of-kutai" aria-label="Kingdom of Kutai on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7931 2168 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Kutai lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8251 2424L8259 2455"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8259 2455h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8251 2424h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8251 2424h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Muara Kaman. <a href="/atlas?year=992&amp;focus=kingdom-of-kutai">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
